class:: Pdup
summary:: repeat input stream values
related:: Classes/Pn, Classes/Pattern#-dupEach
categories:: Streams-Patterns-Events>Patterns>Repetition

description::

Repeat emphasis::each element:: of a pattern code::n:: times.

If the value of teletype::n:: is less than 1, the input element is skipped and no event is produced.
This behavior is intentional and can be used to conditionally suppress pattern output when n is generated by another pattern.

warning::
code::Pdup:: and the code::.dupEach:: method are distinct from, and do not override, the link::Object/#-dup:: method:
Calling code::.dup(n):: on a pattern returns an Array of n patterns.
::

code::
(
var a, x;
a = Pdup(2, Pseq([1,2,3], 2));
x = a.asStream;
12.do { x.next.postln }; // -> [1,1,2,2,3,3,1,1,2,2,3,3]
)
// compare to Pn:
(
var a, b;
a = Pn(Pseq([1,2,3], 2), 2);
x = a.asStream;
12.do { x.next.postln }; // -> [1,2,3,1,2,3,1,2,3,1,2,3]
)
::

code::Pdup(n, pat)::, where teletype::pat:: is some link::Classes/Pattern::, is equivalent to code::pat.dupEach(n)::;
in other words, the Pattern class implements a link::Classes/Pattern#-dupEach#.dupEach:: method, which should not be confused with link::Object/#-dup#.dup::.

code::
//Pdup called through the Pattern.dupEach method:
p = Pseq([1,2,3]).dupEach(2).asStream; // a Routine
p.nextN(6); // [1,1,2,2,3,3]

//Object.dup will get you something very different:
p = Pseq([1,2,3]).dup(2).asStream; // [a Pseq, a Pseq]
p.nextN(6) //[[a Pseq, a Pseq], [a Pseq, a Pseq], [a Pseq, a Pseq], [a Pseq, a Pseq], [a Pseq, a Pseq], [a Pseq, a Pseq]]
::


ClassMethods::

method::new

argument::n
The number of repeats for each new element. n may be a pattern, so the number of times can vary each iteration.

argument::pattern
the pattern whose stream values should be repeated.

Examples::


code::
// the first argument can be a pattern, too:
(
var a, b;
a = Pdup(Prand([1, 2, 5], inf), Pwhite(-1.0, 1.0)); // repeat either once, twice, or five times
b = a.asStream;
20.do { b.next.postln };
)


// using event patterns:
(
SynthDef(\help_sinegrain,
	{ |out = 0, freq = 440, sustain = 0.05|
		var env;
		        env = Env.perc(0.01, sustain, 0.2).kr(doneAction: Done.freeSelf);
		Out.ar(out, SinOsc.ar(freq, 0.5pi, env))
}).add;
)

(
a = Pbind(
	\instrument, \help_sinegrain,
	\note, Pxrand([0, 5, 7, 9], inf),
	\dur, 0.05,
	\sustain, 0.1
);
Pdup(Pseq([1, 2, 3, 4], inf), a).trace.play;
)


// Pdup used as a sequence of pitches

(
c = Pdup(3, Pxrand([1, 2, 3], inf) * 4 + 65);
x = c.asStream;
Routine {
	loop {
		Synth(\help_sinegrain, [\freq, x.next.midicps]);
		0.12.wait;
	}
}.play;
)


// Voss algorithm for pink noise
// note that pattern.dupEach(n) is a synonym for Pdup(n, pattern)
a = Pwhite();
c = (2 ** (0..10)).sum { |n| a.dupEach(n) };
c.asStream.nextN(1000).plot


// Voss-McCartney algorithm: balance calculation for realtime use
a = Pwhite();
c = (2 ** (0..10)).sum { |n| a.dupEach(n.div(2).fin(1)) ++ a.dupEach(n) };
c.asStream.nextN(1000).plot

::
